# `optim::AdamOptions`

Settings of the Adam optimizer: learning rate, moment decay, regularisation and variants.

Read when `SeptimaOptions::optimizer` is `Adam` (as `options.step.adam`), and by the standalone [`adam_step`](adam_step.md). Adam keeps a running average `m` of the gradient (decay `beta1`) and of its square `v` (decay `beta2`), corrects both for their start at zero, and moves each coordinate by `learning_rate · m / (√v + eps)`. `weight_decay` shrinks the parameters, either folded into the gradient or, with `decoupled_weight_decay`, applied directly. `amsgrad` replaces `v` with its running maximum, which keeps the effective step from growing. `maximize` ascends instead of descending.

## Fields

### `learning_rate`

`double`, default `1e-3`.

The size of a step.

### `beta1`

`double`, default `0.9`.

Decay of the running average of the gradient.

### `beta2`

`double`, default `0.999`.

Decay of the running average of its square.

### `eps`

`double`, default `1e-8`.

Added to the denominator for stability.

### `weight_decay`

`double`, default `0`.

Strength of the pull toward zero.

### `amsgrad`

`bool`, default `false`.

Use the running maximum of the second moment.

### `maximize`

`bool`, default `false`.

Ascend instead of descend.

### `decoupled_weight_decay`

`bool`, default `false`.

Apply the decay to the parameters directly instead of adding it to the gradient.

## Guidance

Set as `SeptimaOptions::adam` or passed to `adam_step`.

## Practical Examples

### Example 1

The learning rate sets the pace. The step per iteration is about `learning_rate`, so `0.5` reaches the minimum of `Σ (x − 3)²` far sooner than `0.01`.

@@run@@
```cpp
#include <pluss/ma.h>
#include <pluss/tils/kit/output.h>

using namespace pluss::tils;

namespace optim = pluss::subsp::math::optim;
using Pack = optim::ParameterPack<pluss::Series>;
using pluss::Series;

int main() {
  OutputEngine out;
  out.print_defaults(PrintOptions{}.fixed(4));
  const auto objective = [](const Series &x) { return (x - 3.0).square().sum(); };

  for (const double rate : {0.01, 0.5}) {
    optim::SeptimaSolveOptions options;
    options.step.adam.learning_rate = rate;
    options.max_iterations = 3000;
    const optim::SeptimaResult result = optim::septima(objective, pluss::series::make({0.0, 0.0}), options);
    out.writeln("learning rate", rate, ": iterations =", result.iteration, ", exit =", optim::septima_exit_flag_name(result.exit_flag));
  }
}
```

### Example 2

Maximising. With `maximize = true` Adam climbs: the maximum of `−(x − 3)²` is at `3`.

@@run@@
```cpp
#include <pluss/ma.h>
#include <pluss/tils/kit/output.h>

using namespace pluss::tils;

namespace optim = pluss::subsp::math::optim;
using Pack = optim::ParameterPack<pluss::Series>;
using pluss::Series;

int main() {
  OutputEngine out;
  out.print_defaults(PrintOptions{}.fixed(4));
  optim::SeptimaSolveOptions options;
  options.step.adam.learning_rate = 0.2;
  options.step.adam.maximize = true;
  options.max_iterations = 2000;
  const optim::SeptimaResult result = optim::septima([](const Series &x) { return -(x - 3.0).square().sum(); }, pluss::series::make({0.0}), options);
  out.writeln("x =", result.params[0].items<double>(), ", exit =", optim::septima_exit_flag_name(result.exit_flag));
}
```

### Example 3

Weight decay pulls the minimiser toward zero. For `Σ (x − 3)²` plus decay `0.5`, the fixed point of the gradient rule is `x = 3 / 1.25 = 2.4`.

@@run@@
```cpp
#include <pluss/ma.h>
#include <pluss/tils/kit/output.h>

using namespace pluss::tils;

namespace optim = pluss::subsp::math::optim;
using Pack = optim::ParameterPack<pluss::Series>;
using pluss::Series;

int main() {
  OutputEngine out;
  out.print_defaults(PrintOptions{}.fixed(3));
  const auto objective = [](const Series &x) { return (x - 3.0).square().sum(); };

  optim::SeptimaSolveOptions options;
  options.step.adam.learning_rate = 0.1;
  options.step.adam.weight_decay = 0.5;
  options.max_iterations = 3000;
  const optim::SeptimaResult result = optim::septima(objective, pluss::series::make({0.0, 0.0}), options);
  out.writeln("x =", result.params[0].items<double>());
}
```

### Example 4

AMSGrad with a gentler second-moment decay.

@@run@@
```cpp
#include <pluss/ma.h>
#include <pluss/tils/kit/output.h>

using namespace pluss::tils;

namespace optim = pluss::subsp::math::optim;
using Pack = optim::ParameterPack<pluss::Series>;
using pluss::Series;

int main() {
  OutputEngine out;
  out.print_defaults(PrintOptions{}.fixed(4));
  const auto objective = [](const Series &x) { return (x - 3.0).square().sum(); };

  optim::SeptimaSolveOptions options;
  options.step.adam.learning_rate = 0.1;
  options.step.adam.amsgrad = true;
  options.step.adam.beta2 = 0.99;
  options.max_iterations = 3000;
  const optim::SeptimaResult result = optim::septima(objective, pluss::series::make({0.0, 0.0}), options);
  out.writeln("exit =", optim::septima_exit_flag_name(result.exit_flag), ", x =", result.params[0].items<double>());
}
```

